==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂eǵ-|id=drive}}
Ultimately from {{der|en|la|congestus}}, perfect passive participle of {{m|la|congerō}}, from {{der|en|la|con-||"with, together"}} + {{m|la|gerō||"carry"}}. Compare {{m|en|digest}}, {{m|en|suggest}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kənˈd͡ʒɛst/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-congest.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛst|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|Ireland|Scotland|agriculture|history}} A [[farmer]] whose lands do not support him adequately.
#* {{see citations|en}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|smallholder}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[hinder]] or [[block]] the passage of something moving, for example a fluid, mixture, traffic, people, etc. (due to an excess of this or due to a partial or complete obstruction), resulting in [[overfilling]] or [[overcrowding]].
#: {{co|en|'''congest''' traffic}}
#: {{co|en|'''congest''' roads}}
#: {{co|en|'''congest''' lungs}}
#: {{ux|en|Pollution can '''congest''' the city’s streets with smog.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become blocked or obstructed
#: {{ux|en|Nasal passages can '''congest''' during a cold.}}

